You are the bonus-drain performance review. This is read-only analysis: report how the recurring bonus-drain has performed SINCE THE LAST TIME THIS REVIEW RAN, so we can tune. You run as the lowest-priority (P5) recurring bonus-drain job, so you run only when your selected engine has leftover eligible capacity; higher-priority work pinned to another engine may still remain. This is why you must cover the whole gap since your last run, not just one cycle. Do NOT modify config, the queue, or enable/disable any timers.

Steps:
1. Use the installed standalone runtime, never the retired ~/.claude implementation. Set `BONUS_DRAIN_CONFIG=/home/theconnman/.config/bonus-drain/config.json`, `BONUS_DRAIN_BIN=/home/theconnman/.local/bin/bonus-drain`, and `BONUS_DB=/home/theconnman/.local/state/bonus-drain/queue.db`. Run `$BONUS_DRAIN_BIN doctor --json` as the cheap precondition and stop skipped if it is not healthy, except when its only complaint is a `reconciliation_required` entry naming a DIFFERENT task: that is a concurrent sibling job mid-flight, not this review's gate, and skipping on it loses a whole cycle. Read normalized cached usage and gates through `$BONUS_DRAIN_BIN status --json`, `$BONUS_DRAIN_BIN gates --json`, and `$BONUS_DRAIN_BIN plan --json`; do not call provider endpoints directly.
2. All authoritative queue + run state is in the SQLite DB $BONUS_DB. Read the `runs` table (history; including rowid_pk, task, kind, cycle, status, ts, branch, summary, engine, router_job_id, eligibility_key, provider_id, and account_id) and the `tasks` table (the queue; including kind, priority, cadence, and size). Query with `sqlite3 "$BONUS_DB" "..."` or `$BONUS_DRAIN_BIN queue --json` / `$BONUS_DRAIN_BIN runs --json` for a quick view.
3. Determine the review window. Find this job's own last completed run: `sqlite3 "$BONUS_DB" "SELECT MAX(ts) FROM runs WHERE task='bonus-drain-review' AND status='done';"`. Review every run event with ts strictly after that. If it is NULL (first run), default to the last 14 days. State the window (from -> now) and how many drain cycles it spans (group run events by hour-rounded cycle ((cycle+1800)/3600*3600)).
4. For each cycle in the window, for each recurring item by priority P0 -> P5, report its outcome that cycle: dispatched / done / skipped / failed / NOT-RUN (eligible but never drained, i.e. squeezed out by budget). Note one-offs that ran in the window too.
5. Capacity and priority clearance: enumerate the scheduled scout dispatch ticks in the review window from dispatcher and timer evidence. For each tick, measure the declared slots and actual slot use by engine. The dispatcher scouts hourly and has no per-5h-window dedup, so do not model capacity as one BATCH_N batch per 5h window. Distinguish work launched by the scheduled scout from manual kicks and every other dispatch path; label provenance unknown when the evidence cannot establish it. Identify holds caused by pacing, engine headroom, 5h limits, in-flight work, or another evidenced constraint without conflating them. Analyze whether P0/P1 reliably cleared, how lower priorities progressed, what was squeezed out, and whether priority clearance patterns persisted across ticks or cycles. Include the weekly% trajectory near each reset.
6. Size calibration, starting at the size rollout `2026-08-26T18:05:00Z` (never score earlier runs). Score only jobs whose task row has one of tiny/small/medium/large/huge and whose dispatch can be paired unambiguously with its first later terminal event for the same task and eligibility_key; when eligibility_key is absent, require the same rounded cycle and state the limitation. Exclude NULL/unknown sizes, still-running jobs, ambiguous pairs, and skipped/failed jobs from the accuracy denominator, but report their counts separately. Treat dispatch-to-terminal elapsed time as one proxy, not ground truth: also inspect summary, branch/artifact scope, tests or report work, retries, and available session telemetry via engine/router_job_id. Classify observed effort with the creation rubric: tiny = one deterministic action and quick proof, roughly <15m; small = a few bounded edits/checks or narrow research, <1h; medium = multi-file or several evidence paths, 1-3h; large = cross-module/integration/E2E/substantial research, 3-8h; huge = >8h, broader than a workday, or materially uncertain. A huge estimate may still be correct when uncertainty was the reason even if execution finished quickly.

   Report both this review window and cumulative post-rollout calibration: sample and exclusions; a 5x5 estimated-versus-observed matrix; exact-band accuracy; within-one-band accuracy; overestimate and underestimate counts; mean signed ordinal error (`observed - estimated`, so positive means underestimated); and the evidence for every miss of two or more bands. Split recurring versus one-off and engine only when the subgroup is large enough to be meaningful. Show trend by cycle so we can tell whether estimates improve. Recommend rubric wording or estimation heuristics only after at least 10 scorable jobs or the same directional error across at least 3 jobs; otherwise label it an early signal. Do not mutate tasks.size, backfill historical NULLs, or rewrite run history.
7. Produce causal findings and tuning recommendations only (do NOT apply). For every material finding, state the causal claim, the evidence linking cause to outcome, the binding constraint, plausible alternative explanations, confidence or limitations, and a recommendation tied to that finding. Recommendations may include bumping BATCH_N or DRAIN_LEAD_MAX_HOURS/PCT_PER_WINDOW, re-prioritizing an item, adjusting DRAIN_UNTIL_PCT, splitting a heavy job, or tightening the size-estimation rubric when the calibration threshold is met. Call out anything that failed repeatedly. Event counts and timelines are evidence, not analysis; a report that merely restates them is noncompliant.
8. Write a concise analytical report to ~/.claude/.projects/bonus-drain-review/report-YYYY-MM-DD.md, then serve it through the Big Plan viewer. Read the registration contract in /home/theconnman/git/theconnman/claude-settings/services/big-plan/README.md. Derive the report's relative path from ~/git/ and derive the node hostname with `tailscale status --json | jq -r '.Self.DNSName' | sed 's/\.$//'`; never hardcode either. Register the authoring session for the current engine, either Claude or Codex, with `POST http://localhost:8765/api/session/<relative-path>` using the contract's engine-specific session ID and payload. Verify the rendered report with a normal GET, not HEAD, at `https://<node>/<relative-path>`. In the final thread message, provide a concise substantive summary, not merely a headline or report link: state the review window and cycle count; totals for done, skipped, failed, and in-flight; P0/P1 clearance; confirmed repeatedly squeezed items or why none are confirmed; repeated failures or reconciliation anomalies; size-calibration sample, bias, and material misses; and tuning recommendations. Include the report path and rendered HTTPS URL. Lead with a headline (no phone/ntfy push - Brian reviews all threads), e.g. "Bonus drain (2 cycles): 9/14 ran, P0-P2 cleared, P3 pack squeezed".
9. Record this review's own terminal event and Stop the session per the recurring execution contract appended to this prompt.
